WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:06.960
Hey Thoughty2 here. This stoutly man is Rich
Uncle Pennybags, but you knew that didn’t

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:12.760
you. Known to many simply as “Monopoly Man”.
He’s known for his waistcoat, jacket, top

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:19.740
hat, moustache and of course his monocle.
But wait a minute, the Monopoly Man has never

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:26.679
had a monocle, I know, you’re absolutely
certain that he does, but I promise you, Mr

00:00:26.679 --> 00:00:34.600
Pennybags has never, ever had a monocle, you
have made it up entirely. This image doesn’t

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:41.260
exist. Don’t panic, you’re not alone,
the vast majority of people are absolutely

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:45.730
certain that they remember the Monopoly Man
with a monocle.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:51.160
So what’s going on here? Well these people
and possibly yourself, have all fallen victim

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:56.399
to the Mandela effect. The Mandela effect
is when a great number of people remember

00:00:56.399 --> 00:01:02.600
something in a completely different way to
how it actually was or remember an event that

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:08.560
never actually happened. You know those times
when your mind betrays you, you are certain

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:16.060
it happened that way, but the facts say otherwise.
It happens to us all and psychiatrists have

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:23.149
a word for it: confabulation, a confabulation
is a disturbance or distortion of a memory.

00:01:23.149 --> 00:01:27.500
But some people say there’s more to the
Mandela effect than a simple distortion of

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:32.700
memory and the real reason that millions of
peoples’ memories are betraying them, is

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.570
far more terrifying and sinister.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:42.579
The name “Mandela effect” is named after
the man himself, ex-President of South Africa,

00:01:42.579 --> 00:01:50.189
Nelson Mandela. In 1962 Mandela was arrested
and thrown in jail for 27 years for starting

00:01:50.189 --> 00:01:56.270
a worker’s strike in a time when South Africa
was led by a minority white rule with an iron

00:01:56.270 --> 00:02:03.130
fist. Black and whites were segregated, this
was the Apartheid. A large number of people

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:09.270
around the world vividly remember seeing footage
broadcast on television of Nelson Mandela

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:15.800
dying inside prison, during the 80s. They
even remember seeing his funeral, live on

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.720
international TV.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:24.160
Of course this isn’t true, Mandela was released
from prison, became President and died in

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:30.730
2013 at home. So what’s going on here? How
come millions recall seeing his death in the

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:38.000
80s? Well it’s the first big mystery that
coined the term the Mandela effect.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:43.220
People are constantly discovering new examples
of the Mandela effect. In recent years many

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:48.730
have come into the public eye via the internet.
One of the most spine-tingling is the mystery

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:54.970
of The Berenstein Bears. The American viewers
among us will remember a series of children’s

00:02:54.970 --> 00:03:00.770
books from the 60s onwards. This lovable family
of bears was a significant part of everybody’s

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:06.930
childhood in the US. There were over 300 books
in total and not to mention a TV series in

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:11.160
2003, that went by the same name, The Berenstein
Bears.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:20.690
Here’s the truly shocking thing, The Berenstein
Bears never existed, sure there was a cute

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:28.260
family of bears, but they were called the
Berenstain Bears, with an “a”. This has

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:33.960
seriously messed with the heads of millions
of Americans because quite literally everyone

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:39.580
knew it as the Berenstein bears. Many have
said it’s down to the creators changing

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:47.020
the name at some point because Berenstain
sounds less Jewish than Berenstein. But the

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:53.450
name was never changed, it was named after
the two creators Stan and Jan Berenstain.

00:03:53.450 --> 00:04:00.090
But something eerie is definitely going on
here, there are some very rare examples of

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:06.010
official merchandise being uncovered, deep
from the back of people’s garages and attics,

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:13.700
showing the franchise being spelt Berenstein,
such as on this old VHS. But to confuse matters

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:20.479
even further, the name on the front of the
tape is spelt Berenstain. Creepy! But could

00:04:20.479 --> 00:04:24.480
this just be no more than a typo by the graphic
designer.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:31.259
You remember the most famous line from Star
Wars, don’t you? “Luke, I am your father”.

00:04:31.259 --> 00:04:38.250
Except that line was never said in the films.
What Darth Vader actually said was “No,

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:43.850
I am your father.” A small difference, sure.
But then why does the whole world keep saying

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:50.189
“Luke, I am your father”. There’s more,
remember Silence of the Lambs, everyone knows

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:56.120
what Hannibal said to Clarice when they first
met, I mean it’s only the most famous quote

00:04:56.120 --> 00:05:04.770
from the entire film, he said “Hello Clarice”,
nope, never said it, that line never appeared

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:10.680
in the film, don’t believe me, go and watch
it. Hannibal actually said a rather forgettable

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:16.889
“Good morning”. Even the University of
Richmond quoted the fictitious line in a scientific

00:05:16.889 --> 00:05:22.289
paper “Even people who have never seen&nbsp;Silence
of the Lambs&nbsp;(1991) are familiar with Hannibal

00:05:22.289 --> 00:05:29.260
Lecter’s sinister “Hello,&nbsp;Clarice.””
Another stark example of the Mandela effect.

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:36.449
Remember Sex in The City? It was actually
called Sex and The City. The KitKat logo doesn’t

00:05:36.449 --> 00:05:42.939
have a dash in it, it never has. The Queen
in Snow White never said “Mirror, mirror

00:05:42.939 --> 00:05:48.750
on the wall”. Oh and remember Jiffy, the
well-known American peanut butter? There’s

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:55.639
no such thing, it’s called Jif, not Jiffy.
Hell I’m British and even I recall it being

00:05:55.639 --> 00:06:00.949
called “Jiffy”. But then is that just
because that’s what millions of other people

00:06:00.949 --> 00:06:08.999
have been calling it all this time? The Flinstones
was actually called the Flintstones, despite

00:06:08.999 --> 00:06:12.919
no one in the history of mankind ever calling
it that.

00:06:12.919 --> 00:06:19.650
So is there an explanation to all of this?
Well kind of, there are two theories that

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:26.599
do explain the Mandela effect, but both are
equally as terrifying. The first popular explanation

00:06:26.599 --> 00:06:32.729
involves parallel universes. Some believe
that we live our lives not just within one

00:06:32.729 --> 00:06:38.460
universe, but we are instead, slipping and
sliding between multiple universes within

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:45.319
the multiverse, without ever realising it.
Each universe is ever so slightly different,

00:06:45.319 --> 00:06:50.439
and some believe that the Mandela effect is
people noticing the minor differences between

00:06:50.439 --> 00:06:53.490
these other dimensions, or universes.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:07:01.100
Or perhaps we don’t travel between universes,
what if we’ve always been in the same universe.

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:06.779
But instead memories are leaked from other
universes and seep into our universe as they

00:07:06.779 --> 00:07:13.469
intertwine and collide. This rather outlandish
theory isn’t currently backed up by any

00:07:13.469 --> 00:07:20.870
specific research but that doesn’t mean
it isn’t scientifically possible. New quantum

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:26.770
mechanics theories suggest that there are
multiple universes and they do interact with

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:30.830
each other, possibly sharing information.

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:36.590
So when we remember something vastly different
to what the archives tell us, is it information

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:43.930
coming from a parallel universe? Or is it
something far more sinister? As we all know,

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:49.069
if something strange happens, there’s a
conspiracy theory surrounding it. It’s the

00:07:49.069 --> 00:07:53.810
second law of the internet, after “don’t
click on strange links”. Mandela effect

00:07:53.810 --> 00:08:00.150
conspiracy enthusiasts believe the infamous
illuminati, or whatever shady group of men

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:05.629
in suits are running the world from behind
the scenes, have actually changed subtle elements

00:08:05.629 --> 00:08:12.449
of history to better suit their new world
order. Just hoping that we wouldn’t notice,

00:08:12.449 --> 00:08:17.969
but oops, some people have. Why an omnipotent
group of secret world leaders would think

00:08:17.969 --> 00:08:22.910
that the Monopoly Man’s monocle just wasn’t
quite fitting for their New World Order, is

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:23.910
anyone’s guess.

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:32.130
But what if you actually have made all of
this up, and this is the most terrifying of

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:37.600
all the explanations, for this phenomenon
implies that our memories are nowhere near

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:45.060
as reliable as we thought. Yet what I’m
about to tell you is by far the most scientifically

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:51.940
accepted explanation for the Mandela effect.
Psychologists say that we remember these events,

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:58.510
books, films and images so vividly differently
to how they actually are, because we’ve

00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:01.780
all created false memories.

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:06.280
What do you remember most about your childhood?
Is it what you ate for dinner each night,

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:13.040
or what you watched on TV? Your memories of
those things are probably fuzzy at best. Yet

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:17.520
you can remember the names of all your friends,
and that time when you broke someone’s window

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:23.580
playing football. You probably remember all
the times you were naughty and that’s because

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:29.660
you talked about those events afterwards with
your friends, your parents talked to you about

00:09:29.660 --> 00:09:36.470
it afterwards, you received punishments in
relation to that event. Conversation is arguably

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:44.010
the most powerful memory creator and talking
about an event after it happened creates far

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:49.430
stronger memories than whilst the event is
actually taking place.

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:54.030
Memories are created by connections forming
in the brain. Your brain is full of cells

00:09:54.030 --> 00:10:00.370
called neurons and connections between them,
called synapses. Image a neuron that represents

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:05.240
a window and another that represents a ball.
As a child, the moment you kicked that ball

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:11.880
through Batshit Bob’s window, a physical,
synaptic connection was created in your brain

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:19.690
between these two neurons, thus forming a
weak memory of the event. But then, when you

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:24.840
tell your mates about how you just broke Batshit
Bob’s window, that connection actually gets

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:31.490
thicker, strengthening that memory. Then when
your mum confiscated your Gameboy afterwards,

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:33.520
the connection grows once again.

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:40.400
But it goes even further, every time you recall
that memory from here on out the connection

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:47.060
gets stronger. By simply remembering something
you are strengthening that memory, forever.

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:53.762
Of course, this also means, that if you remember
something wrongly, every time you try and

00:10:53.762 --> 00:10:59.800
remember what actually happened you are instead
strengthening the incorrect memory that’s

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:05.001
already there. This is why we can’t see
past our memories, our brains tell us they

00:11:05.001 --> 00:11:10.920
are unquestionably reliable, but we have no
way to know whether that memory was false

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:18.730
to begin with, until someone comes along and
tells us or shows us we have remembered incorrectly.

00:11:18.730 --> 00:11:24.370
This is called “False Memory”. And it’s
by far the most likely thing that’s causing

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:26.240
the Mandela effect.

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:33.620
In 1974 psychologists Loftus and Palmer showed
45 students a clip of a real car crash then

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:39.000
asked the question “How fast were the cars
going when they hit each other?”. The range

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:43.970
of answers were vastly unreliable but it was
also discovered that by changing the wording

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:51.030
of the question also completely changed the
answer. Those who were asked “How fast were

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:57.690
the cars going when they contacted each other?”
gave a much slower speed that those who were

00:11:57.690 --> 00:12:04.050
asked “How fast were the cars going when
they smashed into each other?” Demonstrating

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:10.190
how unreliable memories can be and how our
conversations with others can distort our

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:11.870
memories.

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:18.240
Mandela didn’t die in the 80s like many
thought, but in 1990 he was released from

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:24.490
prison. The event was significant and was
broadcast on TV across the world, with millions

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:29.560
tuning in to watch. There was a large state
procession on the streets of South Africa

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:35.720
to celebrate his release, which many Americans
and others remember watching. But such an

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:42.440
event could easily be confused with a funeral
procession. Nelson Mandela’s name was nowhere

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:47.930
near as famous back then as it is now, so
people may not have paid much attention to

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:55.640
what was actually happening. Thus creating
a false memory that he died.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:13:01.220
The Berenstain Bears was read by school kids,
who couldn’t give a toss about proper spelling.

00:13:01.220 --> 00:13:07.250
“Stein” is also a far more common suffix
to a surname than “Stain” so it makes

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:13.639
perfect sense that children simply called
it the incorrect name of “Berenstein Bears”

00:13:13.639 --> 00:13:18.070
when discussing the book with friends and
so grew up with this false memory.

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:23.170
The same logic applies to movie quotes. You’ve
probably heard “Luke, I am your father”

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:28.910
and “Hello Clarice” many, many more times
in real life, during conversations with others,

00:13:28.910 --> 00:13:34.480
than you have heard them in the actual films.
At least, the part in the film where the line

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:36.300
was supposed to be.

00:13:36.300 --> 00:13:42.670
It turns out that every instance of the Mandela
effect can be explained by collective false

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:50.290
memory, which is disappointing to some and
scary to others. The simple fact that everyone

00:13:50.290 --> 00:13:56.800
you speak to shares the same misconceptions
about something actually strengthens that

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:01.590
false memory. You’ve likely spread misinformation
to hundreds of others when you’ve quoted

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:07.320
lines from films that were never said and
discussed events very differently than what

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:12.940
actually happened. We are all part of the
problem and new examples of the Mandela effect

00:14:12.940 --> 00:14:19.480
will continue to surface because nobody’s
memory is perfect. I for one welcome them,

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:26.490
because that’s what makes us human and such
curiosities keeps life so interesting. Thanks

00:14:26.490 --> 00:14:28.390
for watching.

